#!/usr/bin/env bash
# .percolate-identity — per-operator identity tokens for the publish driver (coordinator/bin/publish.py) audit.
#
# Copy this file to .percolate-identity (no .example) and edit with your own
# values. The file is parsed by the publish driver's (coordinator/bin/publish.py)
# PercolateIdentity / parse_percolate_identity early in startup; it is gitignored
# (machine-specific). This is the SINGLE canonical home for machine-runtime
# identity tokens (machine slugs, codenames, secondary drive paths, project
# codenames). It is NOT the same as the percolate transform's org-slug
# substitution config, which owns the org-slug SUBSTITUTION vocabulary
# (operator-name → role-name rewrites for the publish transform). Those are
# distinct contracts — detection vs. substitution. Never add machine slugs to
# the org-slug substitution config; never add org-slug substitution rules here.
#
# IMPORTANT: if this file is absent or PERSONAL_REVIEW_PATTERNS is empty, the
# Phase-4 audit's machine-slug detection is disabled. For coordinator-claude* and
# deep-research-claude* mirror/flat-mirror targets, the publish driver (coordinator/bin/publish.py) emits an explicit
# WARNING (and continues) in this case; for other targets it skips quietly. Either
# way this file must be populated on every machine that runs percolate against a
# public mirror target. The percolate SKILL.md Step 2c
# also surfaces a visible note when this file is absent or unpopulated so the EM
# knows the identity-token net is down.
#
# See ~/.claude/plugins/coordinator/docs/wiki/percolate-setup.md
#
# PARSER CAVEATS (claude-klabauter `coordinator/lib/percolate/phase4_audit.py` parses this file's
# three arrays via regex + shlex — it never executes the file):
#   1. The closing `)` of each array MUST be on its own line — a one-liner
#      `FOO=(a b)` silently parses to `[]`. Keep the multi-line form below.
#   2. A missing array degrades silently to an empty list (no error, no warning).
#      If you delete or rename an array, the audit gate quietly stops using it.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# PERSONAL_EXPECTED_PATTERNS
#   Tokens the audit gate skips when found in publishable files. These are
#   expected matches that need no action — your name, your org slug, your
#   working-branch prefix, etc.
#
#   Example:
#     PERSONAL_EXPECTED_PATTERNS=(
#       'mycompany-internal'
#       "M'Surname"
#       'My Full Name'
#       'work/my-machine/'
#     )
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
PERSONAL_EXPECTED_PATTERNS=()

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# PERSONAL_REVIEW_PATTERNS
#   PCRE patterns the audit gate FLAGS for review when found. These are paths
#   or usernames specific to your machine that should not ship into publish
#   repos. the publish driver (coordinator/bin/publish.py) already auto-derives generic patterns from $HOME and the
#   drive letter of $SCRIPT_DIR; use this array for anything else that is
#   uniquely yours (machine hostnames, secondary drives, project codenames).
#
#   Patterns are evaluated via Perl PCRE (perl_match in the publish driver (coordinator/bin/publish.py)), so \b
#   word-boundaries and lookaheads work. Escape backslashes for shell quoting
#   (use '\\\\' for one literal backslash in JSON-encoded strings, '\\' for one
#   literal backslash in raw paths).
#
#   Three classes of operator-private token belong here:
#     (a) Private company org — any internal org slug that must not ship.
#     (b) Private repo/project codename — any internal codename referenced
#         in docstrings, branch names, or comments that must not ship.
#     (c) Machine slugs — any hostname or machine identifier referenced in
#         docstrings, branch-naming examples, or path examples.
#
#   Example:
#     PERSONAL_REVIEW_PATTERNS=(
#       '\bmymachine\b'        # class (c): machine slug
#       '\bMy-Codename\b'      # class (b): project codename
#       '\bMyOrg-Internal\b'   # class (a): private company org
#       'D:\\\\Projects'   # foreign-path-ok: sample redaction pattern — the path shape is the thing being matched
#       'D:\\Projects'     # foreign-path-ok: sample redaction pattern — the path shape is the thing being matched
#     )
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
PERSONAL_REVIEW_PATTERNS=()

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# PERSONAL_ALLOW_TOKENS
#   Tokens added to the `\boduffy\b`-style identity allow regex. If your
#   PERSONAL_REVIEW_PATTERNS catches a bare-identifier shape (e.g. your
#   surname or GitHub login appearing alone), put the FULL allowed forms
#   here — anything in the file that does NOT match these patterns will be
#   flagged for review.
#
#   These are merged into the audit's `allow_re` alternation, so write each
#   entry as a regex fragment.
#
#   Example:
#     PERSONAL_ALLOW_TOKENS=(
#       'mycompany-internal'
#       "M'Surname"
#     )
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
PERSONAL_ALLOW_TOKENS=()
